### Why can static methods not be overridden?

Overriding means: the JVM looks at the actual object type at runtime and calls the corresponding method. This is dynamic dispatch, the mechanism behind polymorphism. Static methods do not participate in dynamic dispatch – they are dispatched on the class at compile time based on the reference type.

```java
package com.bank;

public class BankApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("BankApp main");
    }
}

public class MobileBankApp extends BankApp {
    // This is method HIDING, not overriding
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("MobileBankApp main");
    }
}
```

```bash
java BankApp        # Output: BankApp main
java MobileBankApp  # Output: MobileBankApp main
```

Each class has its own independent main. The JVM calls whichever class you specify on the command line. There is no polymorphic dispatch.

### What is the difference between hiding and overriding?

The key difference is when the decision is made. Overriding is a runtime decision based on the object type. Hiding is a compile-time decision based on the reference type.

```java
public class Parent {
    public static void greet() { System.out.println("Parent"); }
    public void hello() { System.out.println("Parent hello"); }
}

public class Child extends Parent {
    public static void greet() { System.out.println("Child"); }  // HIDES
    @Override
    public void hello() { System.out.println("Child hello"); }   // OVERRIDES
}

Parent p = new Child();
p.greet();   // "Parent" – static: resolved by REFERENCE type (Parent)
p.hello();   // "Child hello" – instance: resolved by OBJECT type (Child)
```

| Feature | Overriding (instance) | Hiding (static) |
|---|---|---|
| Resolved by | Object type (runtime) | Reference type (compile time) |
| @Override works? | Yes | No (compiler error) |
| Polymorphic? | Yes | No |
| Binding | Late binding | Early binding |

### Can you put @Override on main?

No. The compiler rejects @Override on any static method, including main.

```java
public class ChildApp extends ParentApp {
    // @Override  // COMPILE ERROR: method does not override a superclass method
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ChildApp");
    }
}
```

### What does the bytecode look like?

At the bytecode level, static method calls use the `invokestatic` instruction, which specifies the exact class and method at compile time. Instance method calls use `invokevirtual`, which looks up the method in the object's virtual method table at runtime.

```java
// Compile-time bytecode for static call:
// invokestatic BankApp.main:([Ljava/lang/String;)V

// Compile-time bytecode for instance call:
// invokevirtual Parent.hello:()V   ← resolved at runtime to Child.hello()
```

This is why static methods cannot be overridden – the JVM does not look at the object's type when executing `invokestatic`.

### What are common mistakes?

- **Assuming child's main is called polymorphically.** `java ParentApp` always runs ParentApp.main, even if ChildApp exists with its own main.
- **Thinking @Override works on static methods.** It does not – the compiler rejects it.
- **Confusing hiding with overriding.** Both define a method with the same name, but hiding is compile-time, overriding is runtime.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **In Spring Boot, you typically have one main class.** The `@SpringBootApplication` class has main. Subclassing it and adding another main would be confusing and pointless.
- **The JVM entry point is always `ClassName.main(args)`.** It is a static call, not an instance dispatch. This is by design – the JVM does not create any objects before main runs, so there is no object to dispatch on.
- **In a banking project at National Bank of India,** Rajesh keeps a single main class per deployable artifact. Utility classes that need their own main (like migration tools) are placed in separate modules with their own main class.
